#
# iceland-battery-guard: a second safety layer for charging on the OnePlus Pad 4 under mainline Linux.
# On stock, the OnePlus charging stack (oplus_chg) enforces these limits; mainline only has the SoCCP charger
# firmware + qcom_battmgr. Until it is proven that the firmware enforces them by itself (PLAN 5-PM C step 2), this
# service watches the battery and stops charging, lowers the charge current, or shuts down at the stock limits.
#
# Every limit is the Pad 4's stock 11.A.38 value (live stock DT capture 2; KNOWLEDGE rule 13), unless marked OURS.
# The kernel side (qcom_battmgr, Pad 4) already applies the stock per-charger-type input and charge currents.
#
#  oplus,comm  batt-them-thr (decidegC) = -100 0 50 120 160 350 450 530 -> 9 temperature regions R0..R8
#              sw-over-fv-mv  (per region) = 4508 x6, 4488, 4138, 4138   -> stop charging above this
#              wired-vbatdet-mv (per region) = 0 4200 4300 4400 4400 4400 4380 4030 0 -> recharge below this
#              vbatt-ov-thr-mv = 4600                                   -> over-voltage, stop charging
#              poweroff_high_batt_temp = 720, poweroff_emergency_batt_temp = 770 (decidegC); the framework's
#              own high-temperature protection shuts down earlier, at 700 (odm sys_high_temp_protect
#              HighTemperatureProtectShutDown), so the guard powers off at 70 C and forces it at 77 C
#              vbat_uv_thr_mv = 3300                                    -> power off when discharging below it
#  oplus,wired led_on-fccmax-ma (per region) = 0 1959 3300 3300 3300 3300 3300 1000 0 -> charge current cap
#              (the screen-on table, used always: it is the lower one; 0 = no charging, i.e. below -10 C and
#              at/above 53 C)
#  oplus,mms_wired (USB-C connector: stock reads usb_temp_adc_l / _r; the guard takes the hottest valid reading of
#              the usb-temp-l, usb-temp-r and usb-therm zones):
#              stop  : usb >= usbtemp_ntc_temp_low 57 C (battery <= usbtemp_batt_temp_low 50 C),
#                      usb >= usbtemp_ntc_temp_high 69 C (battery above 50 C),
#                      or usb - battery >= 7 / 12 / 15 C (usbtemp_temp_gap_low_with_batt_temp /
#                      _high_with_batt_temp / _with_batt_temp_in_over_hot, the last from battery >= 60 C
#                      usbtemp_batt_temp_over_hot); stock then stays stopped until the charger is unplugged.
#              cool down: usb >= 54 / 65 C or gap >= 12 / 20 C -> 1200 mA (usbtemp_cool_down_*,
#                      cool_down_pdqc_curr_ma level 1); recover below 48 / 60 C and gap below 6 / 15 C.
#              rise fast: the connector warming by rise_fast_temp 3 C -> stop (latched like the other USB stops).
#  VBUS over-voltage: stock's charger OV is 11000 mV -> stop (latched until unplugged).
#  Awake while a charger is in: stock's charging driver holds a wakelock from plug-in to
#              unplug (oplus_chg_wired.c:1872 oplus_wired_set_awake(chip, true), :1968 false), so on stock the
#              kernel side of charging never sleeps. Under mainline a suspend freezes this guard and the kernel's
#              5 s attach poll, and the charger firmware goes on alone with the last limits: no temperature
#              regions, no USB-C connector rules, no 85 % limit. So the guard holds a logind block on "sleep"
#              while a charger is in (KEEP_AWAKE=0 in the conf file turns that off). The screen still blanks;
#              "systemctl suspend -i" still suspends.
#  The cover (wf/wake): logind's lid action ignores "sleep" blocks (LidSwitchIgnoreInhibited=yes, its default),
#              so the block covers the lid switch as well UNLESS logind only locks (or ignores) a closing cover with
#              a charger in: HandleLidSwitchExternalPower and HandleLidSwitchDocked both "lock" or "ignore", as the
#              package's logind drop-in (logind.conf.d/60-iceland.conf) sets them. Then a closing cover locks the
#              session and the screen goes dark, the tablet stays awake (stock turns the screen off and keeps its
#              kernel awake), and at the unplug logind looks at the closed cover again and suspends. logind counts
#              the charger as external power by the same online attribute the guard reads (on_ac_power(): any
#              non-battery supply online; a USB one unless its USB-C port is a source). If logind would sleep on a
#              closing cover, or its setting can't be read (logind not up yet), the lid switch is blocked too and
#              the cover does nothing: the careful side. Read again every LID_RECHECK_S while a charger is in;
#              BLOCK_LID_SWITCH=yes in the conf file blocks the lid switch always (the cover then does nothing on
#              a charger).
#  OURS (not stock values): the input-current check: three readings in a row with the
#  measured input current more than 15 % + 50 mA above the limit the firmware reports -> stop charging until the
#  charger is unplugged (before patch 0053 the tablet drew 1.2-1.9 A from a PC port); a status line in the journal
#  at plug-in, 10 s later, every 10 minutes and at unplug;
#  the 45 C floor for the gap rules (stop and cool-down) (as in the OnePlus usbtemp code; PC verifies it in the
#  OnePlusOSS source, relay 013), 2 C temperature hysteresis on region changes, 3 consecutive low-voltage readings
#  before a power-off (load dips), "no charging while the battery temperature or voltage can't be read", the
#  rise-fast window (one 5 s sample; stock counts over 20-30 samples), a failed stop being retried every step
#  with the warning repeated every 5 minutes, connector readings outside -30..125 C treated as a broken channel,
#  and the charge limit (CHARGE_LIMIT / CHARGE_RESUME in /etc/iceland/battery-guard.conf, the user's 85 %): at
#  or above the limit charging stops, and it starts again at or below the resume level.
#  With a kernel that has the charge limit itself (charge_control_end_threshold on the battery: qcom_battmgr's
#  software limit, written by UPower when GNOME's "Preserve Battery Health" is on) the guard follows the kernel's
#  two thresholds instead of the config file, with the kernel's rule: stop at or above the end threshold, start
#  again below the start threshold. The kernel keeps the charger off while the guard's charge_behaviour OR its own
#  limit says so, so the two layers cannot work against each other; the guard is the second one.
#  (LIMIT_SOURCE=conf in the conf file: always the config file's two numbers, as before 0.17.)
#  Thermal cap: iceland-thermal (the skin temperature policy) publishes the charge-current cap of the present
#  temperature gear in /run/iceland/thermal-fcc-ma (stock's charging cool-down levels: cool_down_pdqc_curr_ma and
#  cool_down_normal_curr_ma 1200 1500 2000 mA, stock overlay dt00.dts:4220-4223). The guard takes the smaller of
#  that cap and its own (temperature region, USB-C connector cool-down), so its own stock limits are never raised.
#  OURS: a cap file older than 60 s is ignored (iceland-thermal rewrites it every 5 s): if that service stops, the
#  guard goes on with its own limits. THERMAL_CAP=0 in the conf file: the cap file is not read.
#  Bypass charging (stock oplus_chg_plc, Settings > Battery "Bypass charging" and its Quick Settings tile): with a
#  charger in and a request of the bypass switch (/run/iceland/bypass-request, written by iceland-bypassd for the
#  GNOME Quick Settings toggle), charging stops once the battery is at or above the chosen threshold (stock's picker
#  begins at 20 %) and the tablet runs from the charger: the same charge_behaviour inhibit-charge the guard uses for
#  its own stops, i.e. stock's output_suspend vote in buck mode (charging off, input kept). Like stock's simple
#  strategy it holds the level: when the battery falls 1 % below the level the hold began at, charging resumes
#  until that level is back. The request ends when its lease runs out (stock plc_support_timeout: 5 minutes unless
#  renewed; the toggle renews it while the session is unlocked) and at unplug (stock), and it is never restored
#  after a reboot (/run). Bypass is only one more reason to stop: every limit above still stops charging by itself,
#  and nothing in bypass can make the charger charge. OURS: a request whose end lies more than 10 minutes ahead is
#  not trusted (clock jump). BYPASS=0 in the conf file: requests are ignored and removed. The guard publishes what
#  it does in /run/iceland/bypass-state for the toggle.
#  OURS (bypass safety, wf/bypasssafe): when the guard stops while the only reason to stop charging is bypass, it
#  writes "auto" (the kernel would keep an inhibit until the next reboot, also across unplugging); a fault in the
#  bypass code ends the request and never the stock rules; a request that is not text or whose end time is not a
#  finite number is not used.
#  USB-C input cut (0.19): at a USB-C connector stop (temperature, gap, rise fast) and at the input over-limit stop
#              stock does not only stop charging: its connector protection opens CC, suspends the charger input and
#              discharges VBUS (oplus_mms_wired.c:2701-2741 oplus_usbtemp_dischg_action(): SET_TYPEC_MODE DISABLE,
#              the WIRED_CHARGE_SUSPEND vote, the discharge GPIO); the input suspend is the firmware property
#              OPLUS_USB_INPUT_CURRENT_LIMIT = 0 (oplus_hal_adsp.c:7785-7807 oplus_chg_8350_input_suspend()). With
#              kernel-iceland's lever (qcom-battmgr-usb/input_current_limit: 0 = input suspended, any other value
#              = allowed; the limit itself always stays the kernel's stock value) the guard suspends the input as
#              well, so that the system's load no longer flows through the hot connector; CC is NOT opened (the USB
#              data link stays, as with stock's own plain "charge suspend" vote) and nothing discharges VBUS.
#              Release as stock recovers (oplus_mms_wired.c:2563-2602 oplus_usbtemp_recover_func(), run at the CC
#              detect change :5207-5221): once the cable is out, the hottest connector reading is below 55 C
#              (USB_55C) and no connector rule fires any more; while the cable stays in, stock keeps the stop
#              (its recover interval is 4 hours, :341) and so does the guard (until the unplug, as its latch).
#              While the guard holds the suspend the firmware may report the charger offline: "a cable is in" is
#              then the kernel's present (the port's CID, kernel-iceland; unreadable = in, absent = online decides).
#              A guard that starts (a restart) and finds the input cut with the cable in keeps it as a latched
#              connector stop (stock holds until the CC detect change). VBUS over-voltage
#              stops charging only (stock UOVP_VOTER output suspend, oplus_chg_wired.c:546). INPUT_SUSPEND=0 in
#              the conf file, or a kernel without the lever: charging off only, as before 0.19. The guard never
#              writes a current value of its own: the release writes back what it read before the suspend
#              (or 500 mA, stock's value after its unsuspend at boot, oplus_hal_adsp.c:9622-9649), and the kernel
#              applies its stock limit for the charger type whatever is written.
#  Desktop notice (0.19): a stop for a safety reason (USB-C connector, input current, charger voltage, battery
#              temperature outside the charging window, battery over-voltage, an unreadable battery sensor) and
#              its end are written to /run/iceland/charge-alert (one event with a sequence number); the GNOME user
#              service iceland-charge-alert shows them as desktop notifications (no privilege path: root writes a
#              world-readable file, the user's session reads it). The charge limit, the float-voltage stop and the
#              recharge wait are not events (expected behaviour). Stock shows NOTIFY_BAT_OVER_TEMP / _LOW_TEMP,
#              NOTIFY_CHARGER_OVER_VOL, NOTIFY_BAT_OVER_VOL and its usbtemp warning in its UI
#              (oplus_chg_comm.c:5721-5893, include/oplus_chg.h:202-228).
#  Failed reads (0.23, OURS): qcom_battmgr answers a sysfs read from the ADSP over pmic_glink; while the ADSP stalls a
#              read fails (-110 ETIMEDOUT, seen in r2l). An attribute that exists but cannot be read is UNKNOWN, never
#              the benign value: an unreadable online keeps the previous "cable in" decision (present decides when it
#              reads; the first step counts as in), so no latch or input cut ends and no connector / VBUS check is
#              skipped because of it; an unreadable input_current_limit never releases a cut (the release waits) and
#              never drops a marker of this boot (the cut is adopted); an unreadable status, input current or
#              capacity keeps the 3.3 V counter, the input over-limit count, the charge limit's and bypass's holds as
#              they were; unreadable battery temperature / voltage still stop charging (as before). An attribute
#              that does not exist keeps its old meaning (an older kernel).
import errno
